{
  "summary": "Consumer-viewpoint validation of a landed feature: a fresh agent that never sees the implementation is handed a realistic task that exercises the new surface, accomplishes it through the public API only, and is graded on the friction it hit (the log IS the signal) plus use-visible correctness (a vision judge over the rendered artifact). The complement to the `review` workflow — review audits the producer's artifact, dogfood is a consumer-use trial that catches what only use reveals: ergonomic friction, missing primitives, awkward composition, surprising defaults, doc gaps. Three media by what the consumer must write: drive (nothing — drive the running engine over MCP), author (a guest wasm component against the SDK), build-layer (a new native cap / kind family / infra API on the workspace crates). Returns an issue-ready rollup; never touches GitHub, never gates CI (advisory + soft-hold).",
  "agentCount": 2,
  "logs": [
    "dogfood [drive]: succeeded=true artifact=correct — 6 findings (papercut 3, missing-primitive 0, doc-gap 3, blocker 0), 0 SOFT-HOLD",
    "dogfood provenance: driver=claude-sonnet-5 attempt=opus/high judge=opus/high"
  ],
  "result": {
    "rollup": {
      "totals": {
        "findings": 6,
        "papercut": 3,
        "missingPrimitive": 0,
        "docGap": 3,
        "blocker": 0,
        "softHolds": 0
      },
      "succeeded": true,
      "buildGreen": null,
      "summary": "Drove the terrain annotation workbench end-to-end over MCP on a desktop engine. Built + uploaded the aether_kit wasm, spawned the desktop substrate, and loaded (in order) MarkBook (aether.kit.mark), WorldView (aether.kit.world), TerraEditor (aether.kit.terra, configured with the mark-book mailbox), and TerrainWorkbench (aether_kit@aether.kit.workbench, with a full WorkbenchConfig: 3 mailboxes, layout, camera, panel theme, console, initial draft). Stamped a Grass disc to give the viewport pickable terrain. Then authored entirely through EditorShell-routed raw input to aether.input: three aether.mouse_button press/release clicks over the viewport picked terrain and appended 3 path points to the draft (server-handled EditorShell->viewport->pick_terrain chain); a click to focus the panel's instruction text field + aether.text_input 'Ridge path' + Enter (key 258) committed the instruction; a click on the Material numeric field + two Up-arrows (key 274) raised the brush material 1->3. Clicked 'Finish mark' -> terra.create_mark created the path mark (selection id1 rev1). Clicked 'Stage' -> world.propose ApplyBrush; first attempt rejected NoTouchedChunks (brush material == terrain material, 0 subcells), fixed by raising material. Re-staged -> proposal_id 1, digest (chunk 0,0; 4851 tris). Inspected via aether.kit.workbench.query/query_result and via Preview -> set_proposal_preview (preview_active true) with a captured frame showing the Stone stripe. Discarded (proposal null), staged again (proposal_id 2, monotonic), and Accepted -> commit_proposal, committing the stripe into the terrain (final bare capture shows it persists). Used default blocking send_mail with terminal reply projection throughout; no polling, no invented validation call.",
      "artifact": {
        "verdict": "correct",
        "rationale": "The captured frame renders a coherent, complete terrain-editing composition: a tool panel on the left (stacked light-green swatch rows on a gray-green ground), a terrain viewport on the right (green terrain blob over a dark slate background), and — the load-bearing element — the selected path overlay drawn across the terrain's authored region as a gray rounded capsule with a bright orange/yellow highlighted fill. This is the final committed/selected state the consumer described (redrawn every tick, so a plain capture shows it), and the tool-panel + terrain-viewport + selected-path-overlay elements all match the expected artifact. No missing region, garbled overlay, black frame, or misplaced highlight. The only soft gap is that no legibly readable instruction/console text is discernible at this resolution, but the primary expected visual elements are all present and correctly composed."
      },
      "friction": {
        "papercut": [
          {
            "category": "papercut",
            "severity": "medium",
            "where": "load_component inline `config` vs `config_path` (aether.kit.terra.config)",
            "what": "Passing the terra config as inline `config` {\"mark_book_mailbox\":\"mbx-...\"} was rejected with 'config does not match aether.kit.terra.config: field \"$\" expected object', but the byte-identical JSON written to a file and passed as `config_path` loaded successfully. The inline-config encode path appears to mishandle a struct-root object where the file path does not.",
            "suggested": "Fix the inline `config` encoding to route through the same schema-encode path as `config_path`, or document that struct configs must use config_path."
          },
          {
            "category": "papercut",
            "severity": "medium",
            "where": "Staging a brush proposal whose material equals existing terrain (world.propose ApplyBrush)",
            "what": "The workbench initial.brush.material default (Grass=1) matched the terrain I stamped (Grass), so the first Stage ran 18 steps, wrote 0 subcells, and rejected as NoTouchedChunks. The rejection is technically correct (idempotent paint touches nothing) but the reason is buried in a deeply nested query_result.failure enum (Proposal->NoTouchedChunks->operation_result->Operator->Applied->stats) and is easy to misread as a coordinate/geometry error rather than 'you painted the same material'.",
            "suggested": "Surface a clearer top-level failure reason for a no-op paint (e.g. 'material unchanged'), and/or note in the guide that a brush proposal must change material to touch chunks."
          },
          {
            "category": "papercut",
